From: EglantinePS5
Subject: [PATCH] nx2: the copy guard is armed before the present's second wait (GrowingBlackBeans' 0919, EDEN_GBB_GUARD_EARLY), off by default

What: with EDEN_NX2_FRAMES_AHEAD=1, a present whose previous frame is still on the GPU side also
waits for its own frame (gpu_behind), and so does EDEN_UP_AHEAD_LOAD_WAIT while the game loads.
During that second wait only the present thread is blocked. The game's job threads go on writing
the next frame's data into pages this frame still reads. The copy guard (EDEN_UP_AHEAD_GUARD) is
armed after that wait, when the fence is already reached, so it arms nothing and those writes land
unguarded. GrowingBlackBeans' audit (his 0918) counted 900-1850 stale buffer uploads and up to 8400
stale streamed uniforms per 5 s in heavy walking windows: the frames where the GPU side is behind.
Our play file (FRAMES_AHEAD=1, AHEAD_GUARD=3) has exactly this setup.

Where: src/core/hle/service/nx2_gsv.h, PresentClientBuffer, the gpu_behind / load-wait branch.

Switch: EDEN_GBB_GUARD_EARLY (his name, so his settings files work), read once.
  =1  with EDEN_UP_AHEAD_GUARD on: arm the guard before the second wait (=3 gathers first, as the
      block after the wait does)
  =2  count only: how many second waits start with the fence not reached
  unset or anything else: off, one cached static read inside the second-wait branch; no other
      change. No change either with the guard off or with EDEN_UP_AHEAD_ALWAYS=1 (no second wait).
Not in the kept-store stamp: it changes no translation.

Changed from his: previous_reached is computed (as the block after the wait does) instead of
passed as true, because with EDEN_UP_AHEAD_TWO the first wait was for the frame two back. A count
mode (=2) and a counter line, from our 11 Nov draft (our drafts).
Line every 600 second waits with the guard on (Service log):
  EDEN_GBB_GUARD_EARLY=<v>: last 600 second waits for a frame's own fence (to present #n): <a> with
  the fence not reached at the wait, <b> with a guard armed before it
Side effects, by reading: the guard block after the wait then finds the fence reached and arms
nothing more; the frame's fence action is registered twice (the second finds the frame ended);
the guard's own 300-present line counts such a present twice.

Risk: low to medium. A writer that meets a full copy pool waits until the fence (500 ms at most);
that can now happen while the present also waits, in the heaviest frames. No new wait for the
present itself.

What a test should look at: one walking-loop start with =2 first (how many heavy frames take the
second wait unguarded: the "fence not reached" count). Then the village walking loop, guard =3,
control vs =1, three starts each, interleaved: HUD-icon counter, flash meter, bright-glitch scan,
side-by-side clip; fps for the cost. Expect fewer small flashes while walking, nothing standing
still. It does not touch the too-bright second after a door (a read-back: EDEN_UP_AHEAD_READ_SYNC).

Origin and credit: GrowingBlackBeans, patch 0919 "guard early" (finding and fix), in
Vampinity/GreenHatBoy patches/GrowingBlackBeans/0919-gbb-guard-early.patch, commit e4dab06.
Not compiled on its own (the integrator builds once).
---
diff --git a/src/core/hle/service/nx2_gsv.h b/src/core/hle/service/nx2_gsv.h
index 510d97a769459e11e7051068e4a2ce38322ccafe..a98e2ff046cc7c84ae11883d0db76f680bc064e9 100644
--- a/src/core/hle/service/nx2_gsv.h
+++ b/src/core/hle/service/nx2_gsv.h
@@ -2134,6 +2134,55 @@ private:
                                         m_previous_fence_id, m_previous_fence_value);
             WaitForFrame(wait_id, wait_value);
             if (gpu_behind || ahead_load_wait) {
+                // 0760 EDEN_GBB_GUARD_EARLY (GrowingBlackBeans' 0919; needs EDEN_UP_AHEAD_GUARD):
+                // while this present waits for its own fence, the game's job threads go on with
+                // the next frame, and their writes to pages this frame still reads land
+                // unguarded: the guard block below arms after this wait, when the fence is
+                // reached and nothing is left to guard. =1 arms the guard before the wait; =2
+                // only counts. The block below then finds the fence reached and arms nothing
+                // more; the frame's fence action runs twice (the second finds the frame ended),
+                // and the guard's own 300-present line counts such a present twice.
+                // Off (unset): one cached static read in this branch.
+                static const int guard_early = [] {
+                    const char* const value = std::getenv("EDEN_GBB_GUARD_EARLY");
+                    return value != nullptr && (value[0] == '1' || value[0] == '2') &&
+                                   value[1] == '\0'
+                               ? value[0] - '0'
+                               : 0;
+                }();
+                if (guard_early != 0 && VideoCommon::AheadGuard::Enabled() && ~w[22] < 192)
+                    [[unlikely]] {
+                    auto& syncpoints = system.Host1x().GetSyncpointManager();
+                    if (!syncpoints.IsReadyGuest(~w[22], w[24])) {
+                        ++m_guard_early.second_waits;
+                        if (guard_early == 1) {
+                            // As the block below computes it (with EDEN_UP_AHEAD_TWO the wait
+                            // above was for the frame two back, so the previous one may still run).
+                            const bool previous_reached =
+                                m_previous_fence_id < 192 &&
+                                syncpoints.IsReadyGuest(m_previous_fence_id, m_previous_fence_value);
+                            if (VideoCommon::AheadGuard::Mode() == 3) {
+                                system.GPU().GatherForAheadGuard();
+                            }
+                            const u64 serial_before = VideoCommon::AheadGuard::GuardNow().serial;
+                            VideoCommon::AheadGuard::AtPresent(m_presents, ~w[22], w[24],
+                                                               previous_reached, syncpoints,
+                                                               system.Host1x().MemoryManager());
+                            if (VideoCommon::AheadGuard::GuardNow().serial != serial_before) {
+                                ++m_guard_early.armed;
+                            }
+                        }
+                    }
+                    if (++m_guard_early.presents >= 600) {
+                        LOG_INFO(Service,
+                                 "EDEN_GBB_GUARD_EARLY={}: last 600 second waits for a frame's "
+                                 "own fence (to present #{}): {} with the fence not reached at "
+                                 "the wait, {} with a guard armed before it",
+                                 guard_early, m_presents, m_guard_early.second_waits,
+                                 m_guard_early.armed);
+                        m_guard_early = {};
+                    }
+                }
                 WaitForFrame(~w[22], w[24]);
             }
         } else if (slack_wait) {
@@ -2335,6 +2384,10 @@ private:
     // Eden's GPU thread has processed the frame. Otherwise the game laps the GPU thread and
     // rewrites command memory it hasn't read yet.
     u32 m_wait_timeouts{};
+    // 0760 EDEN_GBB_GUARD_EARLY: per 600 second waits (gpu_behind or the load wait), guard on.
+    struct GuardEarlyStats {
+        u32 presents{}, second_waits{}, armed{};
+    } m_guard_early{};
     // EDEN_UP_FRAME_WAIT_PROGRESS (common/frame_wait_progress.h): what the waited fence, the
     // split's back and the GPU-side beats were at the last look, and when one last moved.
     struct WaitProgress {
